    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nFive concrete, falsifiable conjectures arising from the LWE development in\n`Catalog/Cryptography/LWE/`. Each is stated so that a single Lean theorem (or a\nsingle counterexample) settles it.\n\n---\n\n## Conjecture 1 (Sharp expansion factor is attained only at the all-ones pattern)\n\n`RingLWEStruct.abs_negaConv_le` proves `\u2016f \u229b g\u2016_\u221e \u2264 n\u00b7\u2016f\u2016_\u221e\u00b7\u2016g\u2016_\u221e` in\n`\u2124[X]/(X\u207f+1)`, and the computational evidence shows the bound is attained at\n`f = g = (1,\u2026,1)` for `n = 4`.\n\n> **Conjecture.** For every `n \u2265 1` and every `A, B > 0`, the maximum of\n> `\u2016f \u229b g\u2016_\u221e` over `\u2016f\u2016_\u221e \u2264 A`, `\u2016g\u2016_\u221e \u2264 B` equals `n\u00b7A\u00b7B`, and is attained\n> exactly at the sign patterns `f_i = \u00b1A\u00b7\u03c3(i)`, `g_j = \u00b1B\u00b7\u03c3(j)` with\n> `\u03c3(i) = (\u22121)^{\u230ai/1\u230b}`-type alternation determined by the target coordinate `k`.\n\nFalsifiable: exhibit `n` and vectors attaining more than `n\u00b7A\u00b7B` (would refute\nthe proved bound \u2014 impossible), or attaining `n\u00b7A\u00b7B` at a pattern outside the\npredicted family (refutes the \"exactly\" clause).\n\n**Formal target.** `\u2203 f g, \u2200 k, ... \u2227 \u2200 f g attaining, pattern` in the style of\n`RingLWEStruct.abs_negaConv_le`.\n\n## Conjecture 2 (Optimal-tolerance encodings are exactly the \"antipodal\" ones)\n\nThe original guess here \u2014 that the sufficient condition `4|\u03bd| < q` of\n`DualRegev.decodeBit_encodeBit_add` is also necessary \u2014 has been **refuted**: at\n`q = 16` the offset `\u03bd = -4` decodes both bits correctly although `4|\u03bd| = q`,\nbecause the decoding window `[q/4, 3q/4)` is half-open. The correct criterion is\nnow proved as `DualRegev.decodeBit_correct_iff` (the residue must lie in\n`[0, q/4) \u222a [3q/4, q)`), and two further theorems settle the optimality question:\n`DualRegev.noise_tolerance_le_half` (no encoder/decoder pair tolerates more than\n`q/2` noise residues) and `DualRegev.dualRegev_noise_tolerance_optimal` (the\nmidpoint encoding attains exactly `q/2`). What remains open is the\n*classification* of the optimal pairs.\n\n> **Conjecture.** Fix `q \u2265 2`. An encoder/decoder pair `(enc, dec)` on `\u2124_q`\n> attains the maximal tolerance `2\u00b7#S = q` **iff** the message gap\n> `d = enc(true) \u2212 enc(false)` has *even additive order* in `\u2124_q` and the\n> correctness region `S` is a transversal of the cosets `{r, r+d}` inside each\n> `\u27e8d\u27e9`-orbit. In particular, if `d` has odd order `k > 1` then\n> `2\u00b7#S \u2264 q\u00b7(k-1)/k < q`, so optimality forces `q` even and, up to relabelling,\n> the midpoint encoding.\n\nFalsifiable by an encoder whose gap has odd order yet attains `2\u00b7#S = q`.\n\n## Conjecture 3 (Arithmetic form of the arbitrary-modulus regularity criterion)\n\n`LWEReduction.dotHom_surjective_iff_span_top` now proves, for an arbitrary\nmodulus `q`, that `a \u21a6 \u27e8a, s\u27e9` is surjective **iff** the coordinates of `s`\ngenerate the unit ideal of `\u2124_q`, and `LWEReduction.dot_fiber_card_general` then\ngives exactly `q^{n-1}` preimages per value. What remains open is the purely\narithmetic reformulation of that criterion.\n\n> **Conjecture.** For every modulus `q \u2265 1` and every `s : Fin n \u2192 ZMod q`,\n> `Ideal.span (Set.range s) = \u22a4` **iff**\n> `Nat.gcd q (Finset.univ.gcd (fun i => (s i).val)) = 1`.\n\nThis is the arbitrary-modulus companion of\n`LWEArbModulus.affine_bijective_iff_isUnit`, and would make the regularity\ncriterion decidable by a single `gcd` computation. The forward direction is a\ndivisibility argument; the converse needs a B\u00e9zout identity for the `gcd` of a\n`Finset` of integers, which does not appear to be available directly.\n\nFalsifiable by a composite `q` and a secret whose coordinate `gcd` is coprime to\n`q` but which fails to generate `\u22a4` (or vice versa).\n\n## Conjecture 4 (Ring-LWE noise growth is subgaussian, not worst-case linear)\n\n`RingLWEStruct.rlweResidualNoise_bound` gives the worst-case bound\n`n\u00b7B_e\u00b7B_r + B\u2082 + n\u00b7B_s\u00b7B\u2081` on the LPR decryption noise, and Conjecture 1 says\nthe factor `n` is attained.\n\n> **Conjecture.** If the coefficient vectors `e, r, s, e\u2081` are drawn\n> independently and uniformly from `{\u2212B,\u2026,B}`, then for every `k` and every\n> `t > 0`,\n> `Pr[ |(e \u229b r)_k| \u2265 t\u00b7B\u00b2\u00b7\u221an ] \u2264 2\u00b7exp(\u2212t\u00b2/2)`,\n> so that the *typical* noise growth is `\u00d5(\u221an)\u00b7B\u00b2` rather than `n\u00b7B\u00b2`, and the\n> parameter condition in `rlwe_decrypt_correct` can be relaxed from\n> `4(n B_e B_r + B\u2082 + n B_s B\u2081) < q` to `4(\u00d5(\u221an)(B_e B_r + B_s B\u2081) + B\u2082) < q`\n> at the cost of an explicit failure probability.\n\nFalsifiable by a distributional simulation exhibiting tails heavier than\nsubgaussian at scale `B\u00b2\u221an`.\n\n**Formal target.** A Hoeffding/Azuma bound applied to the single-nonzero-entry\nrow structure established in `RingLWEStruct.abs_twist_row_le`.\n\n## Conjecture 5 (The true optimal smudging distribution)\n\n`LWEReduction.smudging_l1_eq` shows the uniform window of width `N` hides a\nshift `c \u2264 N` at `\u2113\u00b9` cost exactly `2c/N`. The natural follow-up guess \u2014 that\nuniform noise is optimal among all distributions supported on `N` consecutive\nintegers \u2014 is **false**: exact rational computation gives, for `N = 3, c = 2`,\n`\u2113\u00b9` cost `4/3` for the uniform distribution but `1` for `(1/2, 0, 1/2)`; and for\n`N = 8, c = 3`, `3/4` for uniform but `2/3` for `(1/3, 0, 0, 1/3, 0, 0, 1/3, 0)`.\nThe mechanism: the cost decomposes over residue classes mod `c`, and mass should\nbe concentrated on the *longest* class.\n\n> **Conjecture.** For all `1 \u2264 c \u2264 N`, the minimum of\n> `\u2211_x |P(x) \u2212 P(x\u2212c)|` over all probability distributions `P` supported on\n> `{0, \u2026, N\u22121}` equals `2 / \u2308N/c\u2309`, attained by the distribution that is uniform\n> on one residue class `{j, j+c, j+2c, \u2026}` of maximal length and zero elsewhere.\n> In particular uniform noise is optimal **iff** `c \u2223 N`.\n\nFalsifiable by a distribution beating `2/\u2308N/c\u2309` for some `(N, c)`.\n\n**Formal target.** Lower bound: total variation along each residue class is at\nleast twice its maximum, and the class maxima are bounded below by\n`mass_j / \u2308N/c\u2309`. Upper bound: exhibit the concentrated distribution.\n",
