    "description": "The declared frontier is to **prove the trace-lemma exhaustiveness** \u2014 to turn\nthe classification into a theorem in full generality. Define the class of\nCRT-respecting, polynomial-time-computable counting functions (already sharply\ndelimited by the characters-only boundary lemma: only genuine multiplicative\ncharacters decompose through the CRT splitting, while exponential phase\nfunctions do not) and prove the dichotomy that any member is either\nfactorization-insensitive or reduces to a factor-secret coordinate with\npolynomial-time recovery. The present cycle establishes exactly this for\nCRT-multiplicative weights; the general statement would make the aggregation\nbarrier equivalent to the hardness of factoring within the class.\n\nConcrete next steps:\n\n1. **Vector-valued and higher-rank weights.** Extend the dichotomy to weights\n   taking values in a commutative monoid or a lattice, where \"collides on two\n   primes\" becomes a statement about the kernel of the induced map.\n2. **Approximate multiplicativity.** Prove a stability version: if\n   w(mn) = w(m)w(n)(1 + O(eps)) on coprime arguments, does the dichotomy survive\n   with a quantitative loss?\n3. **The noise-floor principle as a theorem.** Establish the c/sqrt(N) density\n   bound for a general class of N-computable aggregates rather than only divisor\n   probes, and identify the optimal constant. The principle is currently\n   witnessed independently in circle-counting leak densities, in the classical\n   error term for primes in arithmetic progressions, in divisor-sum error terms,\n   in the statistics of Pythagorean-triple trees, and in the birthday bound \u2014\n   all landing at the same scale.\n4. **Barrier-equivalence.** Formulate and attempt the statement that the\n   aggregation barrier is equivalent to factoring hardness within the CRT class:\n   the strongest form the framework can attain without resolving the open\n   problem itself.\n5. **Unbalanced semiprimes.** The quantitative results assume q <= 2p. The\n   unbalanced case has a shorter sweep but rarer factor-bearing probes; a uniform\n   statement covering all semiprimes would complete the quantitative picture.\n6. **Beyond semiprimes.** For general N = prod p_i^{e_i} the aggregate factors as\n   prod_i (1 + w(p_i) + ... + w(p_i^{e_i})), so the trace/norm argument becomes a\n   symmetric-function argument. Does the aggregate still determine the\n   factorization with more than two prime factors, and at what cost?\n\nOpen beyond this programme: the famous factoring-hardness problem itself\n(explicitly disclaimed here); whether an unclassified classical resource exists\noutside the counting-aggregate model; and the exhaustiveness of the trace lemma\nfor attacks that exploit the internal geometry of a counting problem rather than\nits cardinality alone.\n",
