    "description": "**Conjecture.** For every node `(m,n)` the Berggren depth equals\n`d_B(m,n) = \u03a3_i a_i \u2212 (number of \"reflect\" steps)`, where `a_i` are the partial\nquotients of the *subtractive* expansion of `m/n` generated by the three parent rules\n(`m < 2n`: reflect; `2n < m < 3n`: reflect-and-shift; `m > 3n`: subtract `2n`).\nIn particular `d_B(m,1) = (m\u22122)/2` exactly, and `d_B` is a function of the ratio `m/n`\nalone in the sense that `d_B(m,n) = d_B(m',n')` whenever `m/n = m'/n'` (which for\ncoprime pairs is a tautology, so the content is the closed formula, not the invariance).\n\n*The key insight is* that `berg_ratio_bound` (`m \u2264 (2d+3)n`) is the crude shadow of an\nexact identity: the Berggren descent **is** the slow (subtractive) Euclidean algorithm on\n`(m,n)`, so its depth must be the sum of partial quotients, not their number.\n*Why now?* The parent rule is already formalised (`berg_parent`) and the ratio bound is\nproved; only the accounting of the three branch types against the continued-fraction\ndigits remains, and it is a finite induction over the same three cases.",
