    "description": "**Conjecture.** Let `N` be odd and squarefree and let `k` be coprime to `\u03bb(N)`,\nso that `x \u21a6 x^k` permutes `Z/NZ`.  Then\n\n`N \u2212 #cycles(x \u21a6 x^k)` is odd  \u27fa  `k \u2261 3 (mod 4)` and `#{p \u2223 N : p \u2261 1 (mod 4)}`\nis odd.\n\nIn particular the sign of every power permutation is computable in polynomial\ntime from `N` and `k` *given the factorisation-free data alone*, so the power\nfamily \u2014 the natural non-affine enlargement of the attack surface \u2014 leaks\nnothing either.\n\n*The key insight is* that a CRT product of permutations of odd-cardinality\nfactors has sign equal to the product of the signs\n(`sign(\u03c3\u2081 \u00d7 \u03c3\u2082) = sign(\u03c3\u2081)^{|X\u2082|}\u00b7sign(\u03c3\u2082)^{|X\u2081|}` with both exponents odd), so\nthe composite sign is the product of the prime signs, and the prime sign is\nalready a theorem here (`power_readout_sign_law`): it is nontrivial exactly for\n`p \u2261 1 (mod 4)` and `k \u2261 3 (mod 4)`.\n\n*Why now?* The prime case and the whole orbit-count transport theory are proved\n(`cycleCountOf_pow_prime`, `numOrbits_permCongr`, `numOrbits_optionCongr`); only\nthe CRT step is missing.  Exploratory enumeration of all odd squarefree `N < 200`\nand all `k < 30` for which `x \u21a6 x^k` is a permutation found no violation (this is\nexploratory computation, not a verified check).  A single counterexample pair\n`(N, k)` would refute it.",
