    "description": "`Catalog/Bridges/GraphTheory/RipsCoverageDensity.lean`\n\nCycles 1 and 2 are *scale-free*: they bound how much a detector moves under a `\u03b4`-matching\nbut say nothing about how large `\u03b4` and the separation `\u03b7` may be for a sample of `n`\npoints.  Direction 2 asks exactly this.  Cycle 3 supplies the deterministic\n(measure-theoretic) half of the `n^{-1/d}` law in a finite-dimensional normed space `E`\nwith an additive Haar measure `\u03bc`, writes `d = finrank \u211d E`, `V = \u03bc K`, `v = \u03bc (ball 0 1)`,\nand then feeds the result back into the cycle-2 stability theorem.  Again each result uses\nthe previous ones.\n\n1. `IsCoverAt P K \u03c1` \u2014 the sample `P` is a `\u03c1`-cover of `K` (its *resolution* is `\u03c1`);\n   `IsCoverAt.subset_iUnion` turns this into a covering by closed `\u03c1`-balls.\n2. `IsCoverAt.measure_le`, `IsCoverAt.volume_le` \u2014 subadditivity plus the Haar scaling law\n   `\u03bc (closedBall x \u03c1) = \u03c1^d \u00b7 \u03bc (ball 0 1)` give `V \u2264 n \u00b7 \u03c1^d \u00b7 v`.\n3. `IsCoverAt.le_card`, `IsCoverAt.le_pow` \u2014 hence `n \u2265 V/(v \u03c1^d)` and `\u03c1^d \u2265 V/(v n)`.\n4. `IsCoverAt.rpow_le` \u2014 **the `n^{-1/d}` law**: `\u03c1 \u2265 (V/(v n))^{1/d}`.  No sampling scheme,\n   random or not, can have covering radius below this floor.  The `log n` factor of the\n   probabilistic coverage threshold is therefore a genuine excess over the deterministic\n   floor coming from extreme gaps, not an artefact \u2014 which is precisely the correction to\n   the \"bare power law\" that Direction 2 asks for.\n5. `packing_card_bound` \u2014 dually, an `\u03b7`-separated sample (the hypothesis of the cycle-2\n   stability theorem) inside a ball of radius `R` satisfies `n \u00b7 (\u03b7/2)^d \u2264 (R + \u03b7/2)^d`,\n   because the open `\u03b7/2`-balls around the sample points are disjoint.\n6. `separation_le_two_radius`, `packing_spacing_bound`, `packing_spacing_rpow` \u2014 hence\n   `n \u00b7 \u03b7^d \u2264 (4R)^d` and `\u03b7 \u00b7 n^{1/d} \u2264 4R`: the *spacing* obeys the same power law, so\n   spacing and resolution are governed by the same exponent (a distinction Direction 2 asks\n   to keep separate \u2014 they differ only in the constant, not in the power).\n7. `IsCoverAt.hausdorffLe`, `IsCoverAt.exists_rips_shift`, `rips_shift_floor` \u2014 two samples\n   of the *same* set `K`, each of resolution `\u03c1`, are automatically at Hausdorff distance\n   `\u2264 \u03c1`, so the cycle-1 interleaving applies with shift `2\u03c1`; and by step 4 that budget can\n   never be smaller than `2 (V/(v n))^{1/d}`.  Refining the sample is the only way to shrink\n   the interleaving error, and it shrinks only like `n^{-1/d}`.\n8. `noise_resolution_tradeoff`, `noise_resolution_tradeoff_rpow` \u2014 combining 3 and 6, any\n   noise level `\u03b4 \u2265 0` admissible for the cycle-2 hypothesis `2\u03b4 < \u03b7` satisfies\n   `V \u00b7 \u03b4^d \u2264 v \u00b7 (2R\u03c1)^d`, i.e. `\u03b4 \u00b7 V^{1/d} \u2264 2R\u03c1 \u00b7 v^{1/d}`.  The sample size has\n   cancelled: **the admissible noise is at most a fixed geometric multiple of the\n   resolution.**\n9. `guardThreshold_shift_le_resolution` \u2014 the capstone, which uses cycle 2's\n   `RipsGuard.guardThreshold_stability_abs` directly: the shift `\u0394` of the left endpoint of\n   the guarded interval under ",
