    "description": "The rational half of this conjecture is now a theorem: every primitive rational ideal\npoint is approached by infinitely many pairwise distinct curves of the plot, one for each\ncharge `2n\u00b2` (`star_multiplicity_at_every_primitive_ideal_point`), and by\n`tree_spoke_charge_spectrum` those are the only charges that occur.  What remains is the\ncontrasting statement:\n\n  every irrational boundary point is approached by **at most one** curve of the plot; more\n  precisely, for irrational `\u03be` and any `d > 0` there are only finitely many nodes `v` of\n  the tree with `c_v\u00b7\u2016dir v \u2212 \u03be\u2016\u00b2 \u2264 d`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `chord_times_hyp_eq` turns \"lying on a curve into `\u03be`\" into the\nDiophantine condition `|a/c \u2212 \u03be| \u226a 1/c`, i.e. into an approximation of quality `1/c`\nrather than the generic `1/c\u00b2`; at a rational point the horocycle supplies infinitely many\nsuch approximations, while at an irrational point their existence would force `\u03be` to be\nextremely well approximable by the very sparse set of triple directions.\n\n*Why now?* Both inputs exist: `chord_sq_ratio` and `chord_times_hyp_eq` reduce the\nmultiplicity count to counting integer solutions of `\u27e8v,p\u27e9 = \u2212d`, and\n`no_triple_direction_at_pi_div_four` shows the technique is already able to certify\nirrational boundary points.\n\n---",
