    "description": "**Conjecture.** Allow the `k`-th query to have its own radix `q_k`.  Then the minimal\n\"mixed-radix depth vector\" for sorting `n` items satisfies `\u220f_k q_k \u2265 n!`, the ideal work\n`kT \u2211_k log q_k` is minimised exactly when `\u220f_k q_k` is the smallest product of the allowed\nradices exceeding `n!`, and the resulting minimum work exceeds `kT log (n!)` by strictly less\nthan `kT log (max_k q_k)` \u2014 a strict improvement over the fixed-radix sandwich\n`optimal_radix_work_sandwich` whenever `n!` is not a power of a single allowed radix.\n\nThe key insight is that the fixed-radix sandwich loses up to one full query register because\n`\u2308log_q n!\u2309` overshoots; mixing radices makes the overshoot a number-theoretic approximation\nproblem for `n!` by products from a fixed multiplicative set. **Why now?** The fixed-radix\ntheorem gives the exact baseline and the exact loss term, so the mixed-radix optimum is a\nwell-posed integer-programming question that can be solved exactly for `n \u2264 8`.",
