    "description": "Fix a field `K` and a feature count `N`.  Call a functional `\u03a6` a *marginal\npredictor* if `\u03a6` depends only on the per-feature observation marginals and the\noverlap incidence.  Conjecture: for every marginal predictor `\u03a6` there is a\ndistribution over data sheaves on `N` features, with all restriction maps\ninvertible, such that\n\n```\nE |dim H\u00b9 - \u03a6|  \u2265  N/4.\n```\n\n*The key insight is* that `missing_rate_does_not_determine_H1` gives a\n*uniform* family realising all of `0 \u2026 N` on a single nerve with a single\nmissingness profile, so any predictor blind to the restriction maps must pay the\nfull spread of that family \u2014 the minimax error is linear in `N`, not `o(N)`.\n\n*Why now?*  The realisability theorem is proved and constructive (the witness is\n`a\u1d62 = 1` on a chosen subset, `2` off it), so the remaining step is a\none-line averaging argument over a uniformly random subset.  Falsifiable by\nexhibiting a marginal predictor with sublinear expected error.",
