    "description": "**Statement.** Every solution of `C(n,k) = C(n-1,k+1)` with `1 \u2264 k \u2264 n-2` is a member\nof the Fibonacci family, i.e. `n = F(2i+4) F(2i+5)` and `k = F(2i+2) F(2i+5)` for\nsome `i \u2265 0`.  Equivalently: with `m = n - k`, the integer `(5m-1)(m-1)` is a perfect\nsquare iff `m = F(2i+3) F(2i+5)` (or `m \u2208 {1,2}`).\n\n**Status here.** `Singmaster.choose_cross` proves the combinatorics-to-arithmetic\ndirection and `Singmaster.fib_cross` proves that the Fibonacci pairs are solutions.\nThe converse (completeness) is open in this development.\n\n**The key insight is** that `5m^2 - 6m + 1 = \\square` is, after the substitution\n`x = 10m - 6`, the Pell equation `x^2 - 5y^2 = 16`, whose solution semigroup is\ngenerated by the fundamental unit `(9 + 4\\sqrt5)` of `\u2124[\\sqrt5]`; Fibonacci numbers\nenter exactly because that unit is `\u03c6^6`.\n\n**Why now?** This is a genuine algebra/combinatorics bridge: it converts a statement\nabout binomial coefficients into a statement about the unit group of a real quadratic\norder, and Mathlib already has `Zsqrtd`, Pell equations and the Fibonacci API used in\n`cassini_odd`.\n\n---",
